WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.260
What's more impressive than a&nbsp;
Jedi? Two Jedi! But there's one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:07.462
reason why Ahsoka's powerful dyad&nbsp;
doesn't make all that much sense…

00:00:07.462 --> 00:00:12.540
Most of the titles in Ahsoka offer a pretty clear&nbsp;
insight into each episode's themes. "Master and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:16.320
Apprentice" features the uncomfortable&nbsp;
reunion of Ahsoka Tano and her padawan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:21.300
Sabine Wren. "Toil and Trouble" features plenty&nbsp;
of both, and acts as a nifty reference to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:26.100
witchy ways of Morgan Elsbeth. "Time to Fly"&nbsp;
gives us a cool space battle and a pod of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:30.300
space-faring purrgil, while "Fallen Jedi"&nbsp;
deals with the aftermath of Ahsoka's defeat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:34.500
to Baylan Skoll. "Shadow Warrior'' features&nbsp;
Anakin's return in the World Between Worlds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:38.820
and "Far, Far Away" takes us into an&nbsp;
entirely new galaxy. You get the drill.

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:43.020
"Part Seven: Dreams and Madness" takes&nbsp;
things in a more esoteric direction. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:46.620
episode does feature plenty of both,&nbsp;
from Baylan's wish to find the hidden&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:50.640
power on Peridea to Thrawn's meddling&nbsp;
with the Nightsisters' magic. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:55.260
the events don't fit the title quite as snugly&nbsp;
as fans of the show might be used to... which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.260 --> 00:01:00.055
makes "Dreams and Madness" the perfect episode&nbsp;
to whip out a particularly strange Force power.

00:01:00.055 --> 00:01:04.860
Early in the episode, Ahsoka locates Sabine by&nbsp;
using the Force to reach out to her. With the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:08.760
action scenes and plot developments that&nbsp;
follow, it's easy to chalk this up as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:12.960
kind of everyday Force shenanigans the Jedi&nbsp;
are known for. However, this being Star Wars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:17.880
every single application of the Force has an&nbsp;
extremely detailed history. This particular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:22.260
power move is known as the Force Dyad,&nbsp;
and it's essentially a bond between two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:25.500
Force-sensitive individuals. The&nbsp;
most famous Dyadic connection in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:30.000
the Star Wars franchise is the fraught yet&nbsp;
passionate dynamic between Kylo Ren and Rey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:35.100
around which most of the sequel trilogy revolves.&nbsp;
Notably, though, both Kylo and Rey have already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:40.179
proven themselves as powerful Force-users&nbsp;
by the time their dyad establishes itself.

00:01:40.179 --> 00:01:44.280
Here's the problem, though: Because it's been&nbsp;
explicitly stated that Sabine's connection to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:47.760
the Force is at the lower end of the&nbsp;
scale as far as Jedi apprentices go,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:51.720
it should be utterly impossible for Ahsoka&nbsp;
to forge such a connection with her.

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:54.540
"Well, I discovered that,&nbsp;
according to Huyang, I'm the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:58.260
worst candidate to be a Jedi&nbsp;
than any Jedi he has ever known."

00:01:58.260 --> 00:02:02.760
Ahsoka's ironclad connection with the Force has&nbsp;
been firmly established over the course of her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:08.220
many, many appearances in the Star Wars franchise.&nbsp;
In Ahsoka, she's not only a masterful combatant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:13.260
but so powerful that she can handily use rare&nbsp;
Force powers like psychometry. Because of this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:17.100
it's entirely believable that she might be&nbsp;
able to maintain a Force Dyad connection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:21.660
to another person. However, the Dyadic&nbsp;
connection historically requires both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:25.779
sides to be totally in tune with the&nbsp;
Force, which Sabine very much isn't.

00:02:25.779 --> 00:02:30.039
While Ahsoka and Sabine clearly share a&nbsp;
strong — if sometimes strained — relationship,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.039 --> 00:02:34.440
Ahsoka has spent plenty of time hammering&nbsp;
home the apparent fact that Sabine is all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:38.340
but useless in the ways of the Force. As&nbsp;
such, it should be quite impossible for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:42.240
Ahsoka to maintain a Force bond with her&nbsp;
padawan. Of course, that's assuming that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:47.068
Sabine's Force sensitivity is at the virtually&nbsp;
nonexistent level the series has shown so far.

00:02:47.068 --> 00:02:52.260
Despite the show's insistence that Sabine is a&nbsp;
low-end Force user at best, Ahsoka has essentially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:56.460
telegraphed the end of her arc by including&nbsp;
several scenes that tease her successful use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.460 --> 00:03:01.140
of Force powers. At this point, a scene where she&nbsp;
finally learns to wield the Force is virtually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:05.520
guaranteed. With that in mind, the fact that&nbsp;
Ahsoka is able to locate her with her apparent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:11.160
use of a Force Dyad seems like a subtle hint that&nbsp;
Sabine is far more powerful than she realizes. No&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:16.163
doubt, it will be fascinating to see where Ahsoka&nbsp;
takes her as the series reaches its conclusion.

